```javascript
class LRU {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.map = new Map();
  }
  get(key) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) return undefined;
    const value = this.map.get(key);
    this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, value);
    return value;
  }
  put(key, value) {
    if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, value);
    if (this.map.size > this.capacity) {
      this.map.delete(this.map.keys().next().value);
    }
  }
}
```

## What it does
A fixed-capacity cache that evicts the least recently used entry when full.

## The trick
JavaScript's `Map` preserves insertion order. The code exploits that:
- `get` deletes and re-inserts the key, moving it to the end (most recently used).
- `put` always inserts at the end.
- `this.map.keys().next().value` reads the first key, which is the least recently used, and deletes it when over capacity.

## Why `delete` before `set`
Re-setting an existing key updates its value but does not move it to the end. Deleting first forces the new position.

## Complexity
All operations are O(1) on average, which is the point of the hybrid map-plus-order design.

## Caveats
- Keys are compared with SameValueZero; objects are distinct unless they are the same reference.
- No TTL; entries live until evicted by capacity.

```go
func Pool(jobs <-chan Job, workers int) <-chan Result {
    results := make(chan Result)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    wg.Add(workers)
    for i := 0; i < workers; i++ {
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            for job := range jobs {
                results <- Process(job)
            }
        }()
    }
    go func() {
        wg.Wait()
        close(results)
    }()
    return results
}
```

## What it does
Fans out work from a single `jobs` channel to `workers` goroutines and fans results back in on one `results` channel.

## How it works
- Each worker ranges over `jobs`; the range ends when the sender closes `jobs`.
- `wg` tracks the worker lifetime, not individual jobs.
- A dedicated goroutine waits for all workers, then closes `results`. Closing in the waiter is essential: only the sender should close a channel, and only after all senders are done.

## Why the separate closer goroutine
If `Pool` called `wg.Wait()` directly it would block the caller, which is consuming `results`. The closer must run concurrently so the consumer can drain results as they arrive.

## Backpressure
The unbuffered `results` channel means a slow consumer pauses workers rather than buffering unbounded memory.

## Caveats
- If `jobs` is never closed, workers leak.
- A panic in `Process` takes down the whole program; recover inside the worker if needed.

```rust
struct EnvGuard {
    key: String,
    old: Option<String>,
}

impl EnvGuard {
    fn set(key: &str, value: &str) -> Self {
        let old = std::env::var(key).ok();
        std::env::set_var(key, value);
        EnvGuard { key: key.to_string(), old }
    }
}

impl Drop for EnvGuard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        match &self.old {
            Some(v) => std::env::set_var(&self.key, v),
            None => std::env::remove_var(&self.key),
        }
    }
}
```

## What it does
Sets an environment variable and restores the previous state automatically when the guard goes out of scope. This is the RAII pattern: acquisition is initialization, release is `Drop`.

## How it works
- `EnvGuard::set` records the old value as `Option<String>` (`None` if unset), then sets the new value.
- `Drop::drop` restores the old value, or removes the variable if it did not exist.
- Because `drop` runs even on early return or panic, tests cannot leak environment changes.

## Why the `Option` matters
Treating "unset" and "set to empty" as the same would leave a stray empty variable after the guard drops. `Option` distinguishes them.

## Caveats
- Environment variables are process-global; this is not safe under concurrent tests. Run such tests serially.
- Editing the environment is `unsafe` in newer Rust editions for this reason.

```jsx
function useFetch(url) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({ loading: true, data: null });
  useEffect(() => {
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    setState({ loading: true, data: null });
    fetch(url, { signal: ctrl.signal })
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((data) => setState({ loading: false, data }))
      .catch((err) => {
        if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setState({ loading: false, data: null });
      });
    return () => ctrl.abort();
  }, [url]);
  return state;
}
```

## What it does
A custom hook that loads JSON from `url` and re-runs whenever the URL changes.

## Why the AbortController
When `url` changes or the component unmounts, the cleanup function runs `ctrl.abort()`. This cancels the in-flight request so a slow earlier response cannot overwrite the state from a newer one (a race condition) and so nothing sets state after unmount.

## The `AbortError` check
Aborting rejects the promise with a DOMException named `AbortError`. Ignoring it prevents the catch block from clobbering state with an error on a normal cancel.

## The dependency array
`[url]` is the only dependency; the effect re-runs exactly when the URL changes. Omitting it would fetch only once; including unstable values would loop.

## Improvements
Expose an error field, and use the returned `ok` flag: `r.json()` on a 500 response throws or parses an error page.

```ts
type Options = { attempts?: number; timeoutMs?: number };

export async function withTimeout<T>(
  make: () => Promise<T>,
  { attempts = 3, timeoutMs = 5000 }: Options = {},
): Promise<T> {
  let lastError: unknown;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    const timer = setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), timeoutMs);
    try {
      return await make();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
    } finally {
      clearTimeout(timer);
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```

## What it does
Runs an async operation up to `attempts` times, each guarded by a timeout, and throws the last error if all fail.

## How it works
- `make` is a factory returning a fresh promise, so each attempt starts new work rather than re-awaiting a settled promise.
- A `setTimeout` aborts a controller after `timeoutMs`.
- `finally` always clears the timer, so a fast success does not leave a pending abort that fires later.
- After the loop, `throw lastError` surfaces the final failure; TypeScript requires the variable to be typed `unknown` and narrowed by the caller.

## A gap worth noting
The controller is created but never passed to `make`, so the timeout does not actually cancel the underlying work; it only stops waiting. To truly cancel, `make` should accept the `signal` and forward it to `fetch` or the client.

## Improvements
Add backoff between attempts and a jittered delay to avoid synchronized retries.

```javascript
function memoizeAsync(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return function (...args) {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
    const promise = fn.apply(this, args).catch((err) => {
      cache.delete(key);
      throw err;
    });
    cache.set(key, promise);
    return promise;
  };
}
```

## What it does
Caches an async function's results, including in-flight promises, so concurrent callers with the same arguments share one request.

## Why cache the promise, not the value
If it cached only resolved values, two callers arriving before the first resolves would both start work. By storing the promise immediately, the second caller reuses the in-flight one, which is request coalescing as well as memoization.

## The failure path
`.catch` removes the failed entry from the cache and rethrows. Caching a rejection would make a transient failure permanent for those arguments.

## Key construction
`JSON.stringify(args)` is simple but order-sensitive: `f(1, 2)` and `f(2, 1)` get different keys, which is correct, but objects with the same properties in a different order get different keys too. For argument-sensitive memoization, normalize the key.

## Caveats
- `this` is preserved via `apply`.
- The cache never expires; unmemoized, time-sensitive functions need a TTL.
